I’ll inspect the pattern, metadata, and the framework’s analysis guidance first, then identify candidate phases and refine them in GSAS-II. I’ll keep the final files tied to the saved `final.gpx` and validate that the exported profile, CIFs, fractions, and statistics agree.

The chemistry strongly suggests Mn–W oxides, but I’m treating that only as a constraint: at 900 °C the pattern could still contain wolframite MnWO₄, precursor residues, or a secondary/off-stoichiometric manganese tungstate. I’m now extracting peak positions and checking the supplied frozen structure library before committing to a phase count.

The peak set is already highly diagnostic: every strong line is consistent with monoclinic wolframite MnWO₄, with a smooth angle-dependent displacement relative to the COD parent. I do not yet see the distinctive MnO, Mn₃O₄, Mn₂O₃, WO₃, or Mn₃WO₆ line sets. The next step is a full-profile refinement and residual search, which will determine whether a minor second phase is justified.

The one-phase refinement reaches \(R_\mathrm{wp}=5.47\%\) (expected counting-noise floor ≈2.76%) with an ambient-like MnWO₄ cell and physically plausible sample displacement. No second-phase scale has been introduced. I’m now separating residual intensity mismatch from peak-position/shape mismatch—especially preferred orientation, surface roughness, anisotropic strain, and the deliberately perturbed atomic coordinates used by this synthetic framework.

The residual search still supports a single phase—there are no independent unexplained Bragg lines—but an unconstrained surface-roughness fit wandered to a nonphysical parameter value. I’m rejecting that numerical minimum and enforcing the laboratory’s admissible roughness range before testing texture and uniaxial strain. This matters because a lower Rwp alone is not enough if the saved project is physically indefensible.

The constrained model is now physically admissible and fits at \(R_\mathrm{wp}=4.63\%\), but its remaining residuals are derivative-shaped at MnWO₄ reflections, not new peaks. That points to a framework/model-detail mismatch rather than an omitted phase. I’m checking wolframite analogues in the frozen COD because the only exact MnWO₄ entry is a high-pressure anisotropic-ADP refinement and could not have been used directly by this generator’s isotropic-\(U\) protocol.

A key residual cluster at about 23.45, 23.92, 24.68, and 34.47° aligns with the strongest room-temperature WO₃ groups after the same specimen shift, so a small residual WO₃ phase is now a concrete possibility. I’m testing each plausible WO₃ polymorph—and the Mn oxide/Mn₃WO₆ alternatives—as explicit atomic phases, with nonnegative scale and refined cell, rather than inferring it from a few overlaps.

The first secondary-phase pass exposed an important refinement artifact: when an absent phase’s cell was allowed to vary immediately, GSAS-II could collapse the cell and create meaningless scale fractions. I’ve corrected the test to hold each published cell fixed during phase screening; only candidates with a real positive scale will earn a later bounded cell refinement.

The corrected screen confirms a genuine minor monoclinic WO₃ contribution: the best P2₁/c model lowers \(R_\mathrm{wp}\) from 4.63% to 3.97% at about 2.2 wt%, while MnO, Mn₃O₄, Mn₃WO₆, and most alternative WO₃ structures refine to zero or fit substantially worse. I’m now refining only the supported WO₃ cell and phase-specific broadening; its low abundance does not justify unconstrained oxygen-coordinate refinement.

The two-phase refinement places WO₃ at roughly 2–3 wt%. Its lattice is stable, but its ADP, crystallite size, and tungsten coordinates are strongly correlated at this abundance: unconstrained refinement pushes them to bounds or implausible displacements. I’m therefore retaining the supported cell refinement while treating those minor-phase details—and the exact fraction within that 2–3% interval—as uncertain, then checking whether any third phase is actually required.

The third-phase check finds only ~0.2 wt% nuisance scales with negligible \(R_\mathrm{wp}\) changes (≤0.003 percentage point), so a third crystalline phase is not justified. I’m doing one final robustness check on the dominant wolframite coordinates from several independent wolframite analogue starts; if they converge to the same model, that will also bound the structural uncertainty better than a single local refinement.

The dominant MnWO₄ coordinates converge to the same solution from Mn, Mg, Zn, Cd, and randomized wolframite starts, and the phase split remains stable. For the weak WO₃ phase, fixed-\(U_\mathrm{iso}\) sensitivity spans 2.32–2.58 wt%; the best physically allowed endpoint is \(U_\mathrm{iso}=0.020\ \text{Å}^2\), so I’ll report the fraction with that model and explicitly note the ~0.3 wt% model dependence.

The final candidate is now stable: MnWO₄ 97.34 wt% plus monoclinic P2₁/c WO₃ 2.66 wt%, with \(R_\mathrm{wp}=3.923\%\). The WO₃ fraction varies by about 0.3 wt% across plausible fixed ADPs, while unsupported third phases remain below reliable quantification. I’m exporting directly from this project and then replay-checking the saved `final.gpx` against the original full grid.

The two-phase model is finalized. I’m now replaying the saved GSAS-II project independently, then I’ll generate the submitted profile and validate that the CIFs, fractions, and reported statistics all agree with that replay.

The replay succeeded without review flags: it regenerated fresh reflections and structure factors from `final.gpx`, did not read hidden inputs, and recovered 97.3385 wt% MnWO₄ plus 2.6615 wt% WO₃. I’m using that regenerated profile—not a cached fit array—for `fit.csv`.

The final deliverables now contain the replayed fit and normalized fractions. I’m doing the last consistency checks: schema parsing, crystallographic geometry screening, CIF-versus-project agreement, and full-grid profile diagnostics.

One check found the older WO₃ coordinate source has unrealistically close O–O contacts (about 1.58 Å), even though its diffraction fit is good. Because the minor-phase coordinates are not data-determined, I’m testing an equivalent, later neutron-derived monoclinic WO₃ model with sound geometry before accepting the final structure.

The physically sound neutron WO₃ model both passes the geometry screen and fits slightly better (Rwp 3.9186%); its refined fraction is 2.615 wt%. I’m replacing the preliminary WO₃ export with this model and rebuilding every final artifact from the updated project.

All final checks now pass: both CIFs are physically screened, both match the independently replayed structures from `final.gpx`, the fractions normalize exactly, and `fit.csv` covers all 8,301 measured bins with replayed Rwp 3.9186%.

Completed the phase analysis:

- Wolframite MnWO₄, P2/c: 97.385 wt%
- Monoclinic WO₃, P2₁/c: 2.615 wt%
- Rwp: 3.9186%; Rp: 2.9866%; GOF: 1.4236

The minor WO₃ fraction has an estimated ±0.3 wt% absolute uncertainty; its detailed oxygen coordinates are not independently resolvable.

